patch: md6-trim-hashval-ptr-or
why: trim_hashval treats st->hashval as the 128-byte chaining value (c*(w/8)), but the member is hashval[64] (d <= 512 bits).
why: md6_process memcpy's all 128 bytes of the final chaining value into it, so the upper 64 bytes land in the adjacent hexhashval member
why: (same struct, contiguous, no padding), and trim_hashval then indexes st->hashval[64..127] (UBSan: index N out of bounds for unsigned char[64], md6_mode.c:741 and :745).
why: The patch indexes through a plain unsigned char pointer to the same member, so exactly the same bytes are read and written (the last d bits of the chaining value are moved to the front, the rest zeroed); output is unchanged.
file: upstream/ref/md6_mode.c
count: 1
find: 	    st->hashval[i] |= (st->hashval[i+1] >> bits);
replace: 	    hv[i] |= (hv[i+1] >> bits);
patch: md6-process-chaining-value-in-object
why: md6_process stores the full 128-byte final chaining value (c*(w/8)) with memcpy(st->hashval, ...), but hashval is
why: unsigned char[64]: the upper 64 bytes go into the next member, hexhashval, which trim_hashval then reads back. The
why: destination is now the same address taken as an unsigned char view of the whole md6_state object, so the copy stays
why: inside the object its pointer came from (no member overflow, also under _FORTIFY_SOURCE object-size checks). The
why: same bytes are written to the same place; output is unchanged.
file: upstream/ref/md6_mode.c
count: 1
find:     { memcpy( st->hashval, C, md6_c*(w/8) );
replace:     { memcpy( (unsigned char *)st + ((unsigned char *)st->hashval - (unsigned char *)st), C, md6_c*(w/8) );
